Neon's free compute quota ran out on 2026-09-28; a restarted gateway cannot load its data until the database is reachable again.

While this file exists, CI builds the gateway but never ships or restarts it.

Why the running gateway must not be restarted right now:
  - It is serving every shared session from the panel data it loaded before
    the outage. A new process has nowhere to load that data from.
  - Access tokens rotate every few hours and refresh tokens are single-use.
    Every rotation since the outage exists only in that process's memory,
    because persisting it to the database fails. A restart loses them, and
    those accounts' logins have to be added again by their owners.

On the VPS, the gateway's Postgres connections are rejected locally
(iptables/ip6tables OUTPUT, owner clawdh-server, dport 5432) so each failed
database call costs a millisecond instead of ~2.8s. The running binary would
also regress to the stale, already-spent credentials the database still holds
the moment it could reach it again; the rule keeps it from ever doing that.

Meanwhile:
  - Panel changes (new shares, withdrawals, re-added logins) do not reach the
    running gateway, since it cannot read the database. Do the cutover soon
    after the database is reachable again.
  - Do not run `clawdh-server diagnose` against a reachable database: it tests
    every stored refresh token by spending it, and for an account that has not
    rotated since the outage that is the live token the running gateway holds.

Lift the hold only at the planned cutover to a database on the VPS:
  1. Restore the data where the new gateway will read it.
  2. Deploy a gateway that never adopts an older credential than it holds
     (this repo's, from 2026-09-28 on). The deploy now refuses to restart
     into a build that cannot read its state (`clawdh-server check`).
  3. Remove the two firewall rules, then delete this file.
  4. Add the login again for each shared account that was in use during the
     outage: the database holds its pre-outage token, which rotation spent.
